{"name":"Artesian Well","hex":"#5eb2aa","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"artesian well","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An aquifer in which water rises to the surface under its own hydrostatic pressure.","A borehole in an artesian basin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/artesian_well"}],"entries":[{"title":"Artesian well","blocks":[{"text":"An artesian well is a well that brings groundwater to the surface without pumping because it is under pressure within a body of rock or sediment known as an aquifer. When trapped water in an aquifer is surrounded by layers of impermeable rock or clay, which apply positive pressure to the water, it is known as an artesian aquifer. If a well were to be sunk into an artesian aquifer, water in the well-pipe would rise to a height corresponding to the point where hydrostatic equilibrium is reached."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Artesian_aquifer_scheme.svg/330px-Artesian_aquifer_scheme.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artesian_well","wikidata":"Q177734"},{"title":"Well","blocks":[{"text":"A well is an excavation or structure created on the earth by digging, driving, or drilling to access liquid resources, usually water. The oldest and most common kind of well is a water well, to access groundwater in underground aquifers. The well water is drawn up by a pump, or using containers, such as buckets that are raised mechanically or by hand. Water can also be injected back into the aquifer through the well. Wells were first constructed at least eight thousand years ago and historically vary in construction from a sediment of a dry watercourse to the qanats of Iran, and the stepwells and sakiehs of India. Placing a lining in the well shaft helps create stability, and linings of wood or wickerwork date back at least as far as the Iron Age."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Faryab-_village_dug_well.JPG/330px-Faryab-_village_dug_well.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Well","wikidata":"Q43483"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Artesian_Well_%28PSF%29.png/500px-Artesian_Well_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Artesian_Well_%28PSF%29.png/500px-Artesian_Well_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schematic of an artesian well","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArtesian_Well_(PSF).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/US_Navy_091231-N-1572M-001_Seabees_tap_new_water_well.jpg/500px-US_Navy_091231-N-1572M-001_Seabees_tap_new_water_well.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/US_Navy_091231-N-1572M-001_Seabees_tap_new_water_well.jpg/1280px-US_Navy_091231-N-1572M-001_Seabees_tap_new_water_well.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/US_Navy_091231-N-1572M-001_Seabees_tap_new_water_well.jpg/500px-US_Navy_091231-N-1572M-001_Seabees_tap_new_water_well.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"U.S. Navy Seabees tapping an artesian well in Helmand Province, Afghanistan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUS_Navy_091231-N-1572M-001_Seabees_tap_new_water_well.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Artesian_well_Virttaa.JPG/500px-Artesian_well_Virttaa.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Artesian_well_Virttaa.JP